<h2 id="more-links-for-reading"><a class="headerlink" href="#more-links-for-reading" title="more links for reading"></a>more links for reading</h2><h3 id="TSC"><a class="headerlink" href="#TSC" title="TSC"></a><strong>TSC</strong></h3><p>Time Stamp Counter (TSC)All 80x86 microprocessors include a <code>CLK</code> input pin, which receives the clock signal of an external oscillator. Starting with the Pentium, 80x86 microprocessors sport a counter that is increased at each clock signal, and is accessible through the TSC register which can be read by means of the <code>rdtsc</code> assembly instruction. When using this register the kernel has to take into consideration the frequency of the clock signal: if, for instance, the clock ticks at 1 GHz, the TSC is increased once every nanosecond. Linux may take advantage of this register to get much more accurate time measurements.</p>
